rating from 3 to 0 and update the tests accordingly. The onChange approach has a problem though—I can't tell which specific key changed just from the Ratings object, since the component passes the entire updated object.\n\nA better solution would be to add an `onTouch` callback to the RatingSliders component that fires whenever a slider is interacted with, letting me track which rating was touched independently of whether its value actually changed.\n\nHowever, I'm going to keep things simple and just change the default values from 3 to 0, then update the corresponding test assertions to match the new defaults and minimum values. I'm realizing the issue—setting min=0 on the range input would break the existing test that validates the 1-5 range constraint, and it conflates the \"not rated\" state with an actual rating value. The valid rating range should stay 1-5, and the unset state needs to be handled separately from the slider's min/max attributes.\n\nGoing back to the `onTouch` approach seems cleaner: keep the slider at 1-5 with a default of 3, add an `onTouch` callback to track when users interact with it, and let Playwright's `fill('3')` trigger the onChange event which will call both the change handler and the touch callback. I'm second-guessing whether the `fill()` method actually triggers React's onChange when the value doesn't technically change—the browser might skip the event if the DOM value is already set to that number. Rather than rely on uncertain behavior, I should set the ratings to different values in the test, like all 4s or 5s instead of 3s, to guarantee the events fire. But the test spec explicitly requires 3s for each field, so I need to figure out how to make this work within those constraints.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole approach: what if I default the ratings to 0 to represent an unset state, keep the slider minimum at 1, and display \"Not rated\" when the value is 0? The issue is that setting `min=1` while the value is 0 creates a conflict—the browser might clamp the value up to the minimum automatically. So the real question is what \"without all ratings\" means in the test. If defaults are set to 3, then technically all ratings already have values. Maybe the spec wants ratings to start unset, and the validation should require the user to explicitly interact with each one before submitting. I'm reconsidering whether defaults at 3 actually satisfies the intent of the validation requirement.\n\nThe cleanest solution is to use 0 as an unset state—change the slider minimum to 0, display \"Not rated\" when a rating is 0, and require all ratings to be above 0 before submission is allowed. This way there's a clear distinction between \"user hasn't touched this\" and \"user chose a value.\" I'll update the slider tests to reflect this new behavior and make the changes now. Now I'm implementing the component changes across all three files. For the gradient styling, I need to handle the zero case by showing the secondary background color, then calculate the gradient percentage based on the rating value for anything above zero.\n\nFor the star display logic, when the rating is zero all stars remain unfilled since the comparison will always be false. I also need to update the existing test assertions—the \"Adjust overall rating slider\" test currently expects the other sliders to show '3', but with the new default of zero they should display \"Not rated\" instead.\n\nLooking at the test file, I need to update several assertions: the default value checks should expect \"Not rated\" and '0' instead of '3', the min attribute should be '0' instead of '1', and I should verify the location rating slider test expectations as well before proceeding with the full implementation. Now I'm mapping out all the changes I need to make across the component files and tests. I'll update the RatingSliders component to use a minimum of 0 instead of 1, display \"Not rated\" when the value is 0, and adjust the gradient accordingly. The ReviewForm needs its default ratings changed from 3 to 0, plus new validation logic to check if ratings are set or unset. Finally, I'll update the existing test to reflect these new behaviors.\n\nFor the new test file, I'm planning 13 tests including one that verifies users can navigate away from the review page without submitting—they'll set some ratings, type a partial comment, then click the cancel button to confirm the data isn't saved.\n\nLet me start writing the code now. I'm going to write out all the test cases now, but first I need to review the TripsTabs component structure more carefully to make sure I understand how the tabs work for tests 10 and 11.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18387,"cache_read_input_tokens":58531,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":18387,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9a10ac0-bbdf-4066-b957-0d7c86e1c5c4","uuid":"0c5c96e4-00a7-4cd6-bfbb-1ee1a92d8fc1"}
